verses.en:
So lay the matter out along five constants,
compare the sides by reckoning,
and search out how things truly stand.
The first is the Way, dao;
the second, Heaven, tian;
the third, Earth, di;
the fourth, the commander;
the fifth, method.

Every commander has heard of these five.
Those who truly know them win;
those who do not, lose.

War is a way of illusion, gui dao.
So: able, seem unable;
in use, seem idle;
near, seem far;
far, seem near.

If they crave gain, lure them;
if in disorder, take them;
if solid, be ready for them;
if strong, evade them;
if hot-tempered, ruffle them;
if cautious, make them proud;
if rested, tire them;
if close-knit, part them.

A state is impoverished by its army through distant haulage:
haul far, and the people grow poor.
Near the army, prices climb;
when prices climb, the people's wealth runs dry;
when wealth runs dry, levies press harder still.

Master Sun said:
In the business of arms,
best, to take a state intact;
next, to shatter it.
Best, to take an army intact;
next, to shatter it.
Best, a regiment intact;
next, to shatter it.
Best, a company intact;
next, to shatter it.
Best, a squad of five intact;
next, to shatter it.

So the highest warfare strikes at plans;
next, at alliances;
next, at armies;
lowest, it assaults walled cities.
Siege is the method of last resort.

So the rule for using arms:
ten to their one, surround them;
five to one, attack;
twice their number, divide them;
evenly matched, be able to fight;
fewer, be able to withdraw;
outclassed, be able to evade.
A small force that stands stubborn
becomes the great force's captive.

The commander is the bracing of the state.
Where the bracing fits close, the state is strong;
where it gapes, the state is weak.

Being unbeatable is a matter of defence;
being able to win, a matter of attack.
Defend when your strength falls short;
attack when it runs over.

The good defender hides beneath the ninefold earth;
the good attacker moves above the ninefold heaven.
So they keep themselves whole
and their victory entire.

The method of war:
first, measure;
second, estimate;
third, count;
fourth, weigh;
fifth, victory.

Ground gives rise to measure,
measure to estimate,
estimate to count,
count to weighing,
weighing to victory.

Tumbling and tangled,
the fight seems chaos, yet cannot be thrown into chaos;
murky and churning,
the form is round, and cannot be defeated.

Disorder is born of order,
fear of courage,
weakness of strength.
Order or disorder rests on organisation;
courage or fear, on configuration;
strength or weakness, on form.

So one who is skilled at moving the enemy
shows a form, and the enemy follows it;
offers something, and the enemy takes it.
With advantage he moves them;
with his troops he waits for them.

To make the enemy come of his own accord,
show him gain;
to keep him from coming,
show him harm.

So if he is rested, tire him;
if fed, starve him;
if settled, set him moving.

Appear where he cannot hurry to;
hurry to where he does not expect.

You can march a thousand miles without fatigue
if you march where no one stands.

Guard the front, and the rear is thin;
guard the rear, and the front is thin;
guard the left, and the right is thin;
guard the right, and the left is thin.
Guard everywhere,
and everywhere you are thin.

Weakness comes from guarding against others;
strength, from making others guard against you.

By my reckoning,
though the soldiers of Yue are many,
what does that add to victory or defeat?
So I say: victory can be made.
Though the enemy be numerous,
he can be kept from fighting.

So scheme, and learn the reckoning of gain and loss;
stir him, and learn the pattern of his stillness and motion;
make him show his form, and learn the ground of life and death;
probe him, and learn where he has too much and where too little.

Of the five phases, none always prevails;
of the four seasons, none keeps its place;
days are short and long;
the moon dies and is born again.

So:
on scattering ground, do not fight;
on light ground, do not halt;
on contested ground, do not attack;
on open ground, keep your line unbroken;
where highways meet, join with allies;
on heavy ground, forage;
on broken ground, keep moving;
on enclosed ground, use stratagem;
on death ground, fight.

Those of old who were skilled in war
could keep the enemy's van and rear from reaching each other,
large units and small from leaning on each other,
officers and men from rescuing each other,
high and low from holding each other together;
scattered, his soldiers could not regroup;
gathered, they could not form a line.
When it served their advantage, they moved;
when it did not, they stopped.

The way of the invader:
go deep, and your people become one in mind;
the defenders cannot prevail.
Forage in rich country
and the three armies have enough to eat.
Tend your people with care, do not wear them out;
gather their spirit, store their strength;
move your forces and lay your plans
so that no one can fathom them.

Throw them where there is nowhere to go
and they will die before they flee.
If it comes to death, what can they not achieve?
Officers and men give their utmost.
Soldiers sunk deep in danger lose their fear;
with nowhere to go, they stand firm;
deep inside, they hold together;
when there is no other way, they fight.

So, on scattering ground, I would make their will one;
on light ground, keep them linked together;
on contested ground, hurry up the rear;
on open ground, look closely to defence;
where highways meet, make alliances firm;
on heavy ground, keep the food coming;
on broken ground, press on along the road;
on enclosed ground, block the gaps;
on death ground, show them there is no road back to life.

It is the nature of soldiers
to resist when surrounded,
to fight when there is no other way,
to obey to the letter when danger is extreme.
So if you do not know the plans of the lords,
you cannot make alliances in advance;
if you do not know the lie of mountain and forest,
gorge and pass, marsh and fen,
you cannot march an army;
if you do not use local guides,
you cannot gain the advantage of the ground.
To be ignorant of even one of these
is unworthy of a hegemon's army.

Master Sun said:
There are five ways of attacking by fire:
the first, burning men;
the second, burning stores;
the third, burning baggage trains;
the fourth, burning arsenals;
the fifth, burning the lines of supply.
To use fire, you need the right conditions;
the means of fire must be ready long before.

So it is said:
the clear-sighted ruler weighs this,
the good general carries it through.
Do not move unless there is gain;
do not use troops unless something is won;
do not fight unless there is danger.
A ruler must not raise an army out of anger;
a general must not give battle out of spite.
Move when it serves the purpose;
stop when it does not.

You must seek out the enemy's agents who come to spy on us,
reward them, guide them, give them lodging;
so turned agents can be won and used.

Through him you learn,
and so local and inside agents can be found and employed;
through him you learn,
and so the doomed agent can carry false matters to the enemy;
through him you learn,
and so the returning agent can come back as planned.
The ruler must know all five kinds of work,
and that knowing rests on the turned agent;
so the turned agent must be treated most generously of all.
